NAME
       scp - copy files between machines over SSH

SYNOPSIS
       scp [-r] [-p] [-q] [-P PORT] [-i KEYFILE] [-o OPTION] SOURCE... TARGET

DESCRIPTION
       scp copies files from this machine to another, from another to this
       one, or between two others, encrypted through SSH. It logs in the same
       way ssh does: with a key when one is set up, otherwise it asks for the
       password.

       A remote file is written [USER@]HOST:PATH. A PATH that does not start
       with / is relative to that user's home directory; an empty PATH
       (HOST:) means the home directory itself. Without USER@, scp uses your
       own user name on the other machine.

       As with cp, the last argument is the target. When it is a directory
       the files are copied into it; several sources need a directory target.

       On RHEL 9, scp transfers files with the SFTP protocol underneath.

OPTIONS
       -r     Copy directories and everything in them.

       -p     Keep the modification times and modes of the files.

       -q     Quiet: no progress meter.

       -P PORT
              Connect to this port (capital P, unlike ssh -p).

       -i KEYFILE
              Log in with this private key.

       -o OPTION
              Pass an ssh option, such as -o StrictHostKeyChecking=no.

EXAMPLES
       Copy a file to server2, into /tmp:

           scp /etc/hosts root@server2:/tmp/

       Fetch a file from server2 into the current directory:

           scp student@server2.lab.example.com:report.txt .

       Copy a whole directory, keeping times and modes:

           scp -rp /srv/web root@server2:/srv/

       Copy to an SSH server that listens on port 2222:

           scp -P 2222 notes.txt student@server2:

SEE ALSO
       ssh(1), sftp(1), rsync(1), ssh-keygen(1), ssh-copy-id(1)
